{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And you shall command.</b> After finishing all the commandments of the work of the Tabernacle, He explained to him how they should prepare light for it. So too we find in the account of Creation: after all matters of the earth were created, He prepared light for them. And the essential commandment is not here until the pericope of בהעלותך. And this is what it says: <b>And you shall command</b> in the future, when the Tabernacle is built — <b>they shall take to you oil — you shall command the children of Israel.</b> It repeats the language of command here, unlike the language of the other commandments, because it is for a long time. This is what our Rabbis said: wherever צווי is stated, it is nothing but an exhortation, immediately and for all generations." "<b>They shall take to you.</b> For your need, so that you may see where you enter and exit, and not for My sake — I have no need for its light. A proof for the matter: 'outside the curtain that is over the testimony' — he shall arrange it; but above it writes 'they shall take to Me a contribution' — to My name, for My name's sake." "<b>Oil of olive, pure, beaten.</b> Because the verse stated its words briefly in the pericope of the contribution, it explains to you here which oil. <b>Pure.</b> Rashi explained: without dregs, as we learned in Menaḥot: 'from the berry at the top of the olive' etc. Below, Rashi: 'he takes and gathers the berry' — here one and here one, because it is not yet cooked — at the top of the olive, where they ripen first, because the sun beats upon them and ripens them."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:00:49.873140504Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}